l "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(4)q(4)+p(-4)q(-4)+p(-5)q(-5) \\\\  & =(k+4)(1+4-16)+(k-4)(1-4-16)+(k-5)(1-5-25) \\\\  & =(-11k-44)+(-19k+76)+(-29k+145) \\\\  & =-59k+177\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ -59k+177 & =0 \\\\ 59k & =177 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97363" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "93f46b449376cefaf77dd465a16c29685f2f7472b888ef2bccc4151a27c1076d"} {"question" "q-97363" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97363" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-6"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97363" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97362" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(-1)y(-1)+x(1)y(1)+x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\nIf $p(t)=2+kt$ and $q(t)=t-{t}^{2}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97362" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(-1)q(-1)+p(1)q(1)+p(2)q(2) \\\\  & =(2-k)(-1-1)+(2+k)(1-1)+(2+2k)(2-4) \\\\  & =(-4+2k)+0+(-4-4k) \\\\  & =-8-2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ -8-2k & =0 \\\\ 2k & =-8 \\\\ k & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97362" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "93f46b449376cefaf77dd465a16c29685f2f7472b888ef2bccc4151a27c1076d"} {"question" "q-97362" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97362" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "-6"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97362" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97439" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)={e}^{{t}^{2}}+k$ and $y(t)=t$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97439" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{{t}^{2}}+k)t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t{e}^{{t}^{2}}+kt)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{{t}^{2}}}{2}+\\frac{k{t}^{2}}{2}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{e+k-1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{e+k-1}{2} & =0 \\\\ e+k-1 & =0 \\\\ k & =1-e\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97439" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "93f46b449376cefaf77dd465a16c29685f2f7472b888ef2bccc4151a27c1076d"} {"question" "q-97439" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97439" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1-e"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{e}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}"] ["math" "{e}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97439" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1-e"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97436" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=t-2{t}^{2}$ and $y(t)=t+k$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97436" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-2{t}^{2})(t+k)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(kt+(1-2k){t}^{2}-2{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{k{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(1-2k){t}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k-1}{2}+\\frac{1-2k}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k-1}{2}+\\frac{1-2k}{3} & =0 \\\\ 3(k-1)+2(1-2k) & =0 \\\\ 3k-3+2-4k & =0 \\\\ -1-k & =0 \\\\ k & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97436" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "93f46b449376cefaf77dd465a16c29685f2f7472b888ef2bccc4151a27c1076d"} {"question" "q-97436" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97436" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97436" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97440" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=5{t}^{2}-2$ and $y(t)=k+3{t}^{2}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97440" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(5{t}^{2}-2)(k+3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(15{t}^{4}+(5k-6){t}^{2}-2k)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(3{t}^{5}+\\frac{(5k-6){t}^{3}}{3}-2kt){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{(5k-6)}{3}-2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 3+\\frac{(5k-6)}{3}-2k & =0 \\\\ 9+(5k-6)-6k & =0 \\\\ 3-k & =0 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97440" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "93f46b449376cefaf77dd465a16c29685f2f7472b888ef2bccc4151a27c1076d"} {"question" "q-97440" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97440" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97440" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-44093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,\\pi ]\\,,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=1$ and $y(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-k$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-44093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-k)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-kt){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\pi k-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =-\\pi k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\pi k & =0 \\\\ k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-44093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "93f46b449376cefaf77dd465a16c29685f2f7472b888ef2bccc4151a27c1076d"} {"question" "q-44093" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-44093" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\pi }"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-44093" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-43979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=t$ and $y(t)={t}^{2}+k$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-43979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t({t}^{2}+k)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{3}+kt)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{k{t}^{2}}{2}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{k}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{k}{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{2} & =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-43979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "93f46b449376cefaf77dd465a16c29685f2f7472b888ef2bccc4151a27c1076d"} {"question" "q-43979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-43979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-43979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97445" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,3]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,3],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=t$ and $g(t)={e}^{t},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2{e}^{3}+1$\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=1$\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97445" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{3}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{0}^{3}-{\\int }_{0}^{3}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t{e}^{t}){|}_{0}^{3}-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(3{e}^{3}-0{e}^{0})-{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =3{e}^{3}-({e}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =2{e}^{3}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97445" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "93f46b449376cefaf77dd465a16c29685f2f7472b888ef2bccc4151a27c1076d"} {"question" "q-97445" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97445" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97445" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97452" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[1,{e}^{2}]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[1,{e}^{2}],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $g(t)=\\frac{1}{t},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2$\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97452" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97452" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "93f46b449376cefaf77dd465a16c29685f2f7472b888ef2bccc4151a27c1076d"} {"question" "q-97452" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97452" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97452" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97451" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,2\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2\\pi $\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )).$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97451" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the given identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-3t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)}{4}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\pi )}{4}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97451" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "93f46b449376cefaf77dd465a16c29685f2f7472b888ef2bccc4151a27c1076d"} {"question" "q-97451" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97451" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/referenc