Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97448" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "93f46b449376cefaf77dd465a16c29685f2f7472b888ef2bccc4151a27c1076d"} {"question" "q-97448" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97448" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-97448" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-44058" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=\\frac{1}{2}$\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-44058" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-44058" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "93f46b449376cefaf77dd465a16c29685f2f7472b888ef2bccc4151a27c1076d"} {"question" "q-44058" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-44058" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "q-44058" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/state.json" "file_sha256" "6a16d55411c15b12569d8cfef2cc3d047c6c28c7398419f768ec62abfa94729e"} {"question" "e-8581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 3 x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 2 x_3 y_3.\n$$\n\nGiven that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/assets/b8ece1b03ba237390f26e8d47e40013abc221edcae144ca3a9dc233438d486a3.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/assets/febc66857330a11bcd7bb93952cf5b86373974f5bf87690fdcf526d4939f19ec.png) are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V,$ find the value of $k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/example-8581.json" "file_sha256" "6ef98242754969c95a6d533aa41afc417ea8c7b4ded5b08001e323bd29437c93"} {"question" "e-8581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $\\mathbf x$ and $\\mathbf y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (2\\cdot k)+1\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot (2\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =6k-2-4 \\\\  & =6k-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ 6k-6 & =0 \\\\ 6k & =6 \\\\ k & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/example-8581.json" "file_sha256" "6ef98242754969c95a6d533aa41afc417ea8c7b4ded5b08001e323bd29437c93"} {"question" "e-8579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle {x(t)}, {y(t)} \\rangle   = x(0)y(0) + x(1)y(1) + x(-1)y(-1).\n$$\n\nGiven that $p(t) = 1 + t + t^2$ and $q(t) = kt - t^2$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V,$ find the value of $k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/example-8579.json" "file_sha256" "1f4f9ddac530570f2c7eb4c149eab446b0f1ad541465ff7f44d85c38f5198755"} {"question" "e-8579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)+p(-1)q(-1) \\\\  & =(1)(0)+(3)(k-1)+(1)(-k-1) \\\\  & =0+(3k-3)+(-k-1) \\\\  & =2k-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\\langle p(t), q(t) \\rangle = 0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2k-4 & =0 \\\\ 2k & =4 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/example-8579.json" "file_sha256" "1f4f9ddac530570f2c7eb4c149eab446b0f1ad541465ff7f44d85c38f5198755"} {"question" "e-8580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,1\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,1\\right],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven that $x(t) = kt^2+3t$ and $y(t) = t$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V,$ find the value of $k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/example-8580.json" "file_sha256" "57d3982ea0fd43528e33bc31b13e9fc8bab369e196ff8d211d3da41e8cafae21"} {"question" "e-8580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(k{t}^{2}+3t)(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(k{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{k{t}^{4}}{4}+{t}^{3}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{4}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\\langle x(t), y(t) \\rangle = 0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{4}+1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{4} & =-1 \\\\ k & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/example-8580.json" "file_sha256" "57d3982ea0fd43528e33bc31b13e9fc8bab369e196ff8d211d3da41e8cafae21"} {"question" "e-8582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,2\\pi\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,2\\pi\\right],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle f(t),g(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(t) g(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t) = \\cos t$ and $g(t)=\\sin 2t,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle = 0$\n\n- $\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n- $f(t) \\perp g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity* $\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\beta=\\dfrac12(\\sin(\\alpha-\\beta)+\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta)).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/example-8582.json" "file_sha256" "8377b307445d14bda59dc5dd7cae661f80dc064d47fcaae8d76d186dbfa40726"} {"question" "e-8582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle  =  \\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\\cos{t} \\sin2t \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the given identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{3}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi )}{3}]-\\frac{1}{2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-1-\\frac{1}{3}]-\\frac{1}{2}[-1-\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3})-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors are orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/example-8582.json" "file_sha256" "8377b307445d14bda59dc5dd7cae661f80dc064d47fcaae8d76d186dbfa40726"}] "usage" {"q-97355" [] "q-97451" [] "e-8580" [] "q-140263" [] "q-44058" [] "e-8582" [] "q-97440" [] "q-97445" [] "e-8581" [] "q-97353" [] "e-8579" [] "q-43979" []} "basis" 1853 "retractions" [[17592186134473 :question/problem "Let $V = R^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$⟨x, y⟩ = x_{1}y_{1} + 5x_{2}y_{2} + 2x_{3}y_{3}$.\nIf $x = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨ \\cdot, \\cdot ⟩$ in $V$, then $k =$"] [17592186134477 :question/problem "Let $V = C[0, 2π]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0, 2π]$, equipped with the inner product\n\n$⟨f(t), g(t)⟩ = ∫_{0}^{2π}f(t)g(t)dt$.\n\nGiven the functions $f(t) = \\cos t$ and $g(t) = \\cos (3t)$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $⟨f(t), g(t)⟩ = 0$\n2. $⟨f(t), g(t)⟩ = 2π$\n3. $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨ \\cdot, \\cdot ⟩$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $\\cos α\\cos β = \\frac{1}{2}(\\cos (α - β) + \\cos (α + β))$.*"] [17592186133607 :question/problem "Let $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 3 x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 2 x_3 y_3.\n$$\n\nGiven that $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -2 \\\\